And he has all the licorice, right, for them?

1306
00:49:27.240 --> 00:49:30.380
Phyllis is like, mm-hmm, money bags must be nice.

1307
00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:31.380
Oh, boy.

1308
00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:35.860
Andy said, so you effectively spent, what, $4 on the entire office?

1309
00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:38.560
And Jim's like, I can't get a handle on it.

1310
00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:39.680
Am I cheap or rich?

1311
00:49:39.800 --> 00:49:40.280
I can't.

1312
00:49:40.460 --> 00:49:42.020
And then Andy's like, you're nothing.

1313
00:49:42.180 --> 00:49:49.220
When I think about all the gas money I spent on you washing my car to make sure he, like, Andy is just so determined to keep his lie going.

1314
00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:49.940
Yes.

1315
00:49:49.940 --> 00:49:52.740
Jim interrupts him and says, enjoy the licorice, everyone.

1316
00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:57.920
Everyone continues giving Jim a lot of grief about this red licorice stunt, right?

1317
00:49:58.000 --> 00:49:59.520
They're all turning on him.

1318
00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:01.580
Creed, Stanley, Daryl.

1319
00:50:01.820 --> 00:50:05.800
In the next scene, Jim has gone down to Dwight's gym for muscles.

1320
00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:06.920
Oh, wow.

1321
00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:10.560
He's riding the bicycle and eating red licorice by himself.

1322
00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:13.760
He calls Pam and says, they hate me, Pam.

1323
00:50:14.500 --> 00:50:16.120
Pam says, they don't hate you.

1324
00:50:16.520 --> 00:50:17.740
Jim goes, no, it's bad.

1325
00:50:18.080 --> 00:50:19.780
Daryl canceled go-karting.

1326
00:50:19.940 --> 00:50:23.120
Pam goes, when were you going go-karting with Daryl?

1327
00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:25.100
Jim says, it's a lunch thing.

1328
00:50:25.580 --> 00:50:27.360
Pam says, all right, just don't worry.

1329
00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:28.260
We'll figure this out.

1330
00:50:28.680 --> 00:50:31.120
Jim says, I feel like a stranger in my own country.

1331
00:50:31.420 --> 00:50:33.060
Pam says, okay, drama.

1332
00:50:33.820 --> 00:50:36.860
And then Jim goes, hey, you're not here.

1333
00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:37.860
You don't know, okay?

1334
00:50:37.980 --> 00:50:38.540
It's intense.

1335
00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:40.800
They made me eat black licorice.

1336
00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:41.880
Wow.

1337
00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:45.980
And then I guess Pam's plan is let's bring the kids.

1338
00:50:46.200 --> 00:50:47.340
Oh, my gosh.

1339
00:50:47.620 --> 00:50:50.480
But I thought it was really funny when you go, okay, drama.

1340
00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:56.630
Well, back at the hospital, Angela's going to come clean.

1341
00:50:56.630 --> 00:50:58.810
So Jim has come clean in the office.

1342
00:50:59.050 --> 00:51:00.270
Now Angela's going to come clean.

1343
00:51:00.430 --> 00:51:05.590
She's going to tell everybody, listen, this baby was conceived nine months ago.

1344
00:51:05.590 --> 00:51:12.190
So, despite our intention to wait, but you'd just seen Thor and there was too much wine

1345
00:51:12.190 --> 00:51:13.290
in her chicken piccata.

1346
00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:14.150
Chicken marsala.

1347
00:51:15.090 --> 00:51:15.390
Okay.

1348
00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:20.010
Fan catch from Alex P and Katie G and many others.

1349
00:51:20.690 --> 00:51:24.990
When Angela finally admits that she and the senator conceived Philip out of wedlock,

1350
00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:27.690
she says there was too much wine in her chicken.

1351
00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:30.050
But isn't Angela a vegetarian?

1352
00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:32.270
Great catch.

1353
00:51:32.530 --> 00:51:33.290
Great catch.

1354
00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:34.030
Mm-hmm.

1355
00:51:34.490 --> 00:51:39.390
This scene does have one of my favorite lines in the entire episode, which is when Oscar says,

1356
00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:43.170
the only premature baby in this room is the baby this baby ate.

1357
00:51:43.470 --> 00:51:45.830
Well, guess who's going to come meet this baby now?

1358
00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:46.870
Dwight.

1359
00:51:47.230 --> 00:51:49.250
He's come to the hospital looking for Gabe.

1360
00:51:49.590 --> 00:51:49.790
Yeah.

1361
00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:53.950
He's not even interested in seeing the baby until Oscar tells him,

1362
00:51:54.560 --> 00:51:56.350
I promised I wouldn't say anything.

1363
00:51:56.350 --> 00:51:57.930
And then he goes, so don't.

1364
00:51:58.070 --> 00:51:58.490
I know.

1365
00:51:58.570 --> 00:51:59.250
I loved that.

1366
00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:00.930
And then Oscar tells him.

1367
00:52:01.050 --> 00:52:03.830
And then Oscar goes, I didn't tell you.

1368
00:52:03.950 --> 00:52:05.350
And Dwight goes, yes, you did.

1369
00:52:05.830 --> 00:52:07.830
That made me laugh so hard.

1370
00:52:08.630 --> 00:52:12.790
Well, Dwight barges into Angela's hospital room, demands to see the baby.

1371
00:52:13.290 --> 00:52:18.790
I spotted a little something on the whiteboard as Dwight was entering the room.

1372
00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:21.530
There's a little hospital whiteboard, you know.

1373
00:52:21.530 --> 00:52:21.710
Yeah.

1374
00:52:21.810 --> 00:52:25.830
And it says that Angela's nurse's name is Ruth Schur.

1375
00:52:26.350 --> 00:52:28.430
So I asked Aaron Schur, our writer, about it.

1376
00:52:28.490 --> 00:52:30.450
And he said that was his wife at the time.

1377
00:52:30.570 --> 00:52:36.230
She was becoming a nurse at the age of 50 and that she has now gone on to become a family

1378
00:52:36.230 --> 00:52:37.190
nurse practitioner.

1379
00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:39.610
And he was just very proud.

1380
00:52:39.610 --> 00:52:42.670
So he put her name up on the board as Angela's nurse.

1381
00:52:42.670 --> 00:52:43.470
I love it.

1382
00:52:43.870 --> 00:52:49.210
So now Dwight is looking at little baby Philip Halstead Lipton.

1383
00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:55.330
Halstead, which was one of our writers, I said to Halstead, did you pitch to have your

1384
00:52:55.330 --> 00:52:56.390
name in this episode?

1385
00:52:56.490 --> 00:52:57.290
He goes, no.

1386
00:52:57.990 --> 00:52:59.250
Quite the opposite.

1387
00:52:59.430 --> 00:53:00.870
I was like, no, you guys.

1388
00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:02.950
But like the writer's room insisted.

1389
00:53:03.830 --> 00:53:07.670
When Dwight sees the baby, I thought the baby looked like Dwight.

1390
00:53:07.670 --> 00:53:10.030
They did a great job casting these babies.

1391
00:53:10.330 --> 00:53:10.950
It's true.

1392
00:53:11.250 --> 00:53:11.490
Yeah.

1393
00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:16.490
Although in a later scene, Angela is going to say, your face looks like a baby, Dwight.

1394
00:53:16.490 --> 00:53:18.370
That was one of my favorite lines to say.

1395
00:53:18.630 --> 00:53:21.650
So maybe any baby looks like Rainn Wilson.

1396
00:53:21.890 --> 00:53:22.190
Maybe.

1397
00:53:22.670 --> 00:53:23.630
But they did a good job.

1398
00:53:23.910 --> 00:53:26.310
I think there's three categories of babies.

1399
00:53:26.590 --> 00:53:27.110
Oh, yeah?

1400
00:53:27.350 --> 00:53:27.850
Yes.

1401
00:53:28.150 --> 00:53:30.610
There are chubber babies.

1402
00:53:30.770 --> 00:53:31.130
Uh-huh.

1403
00:53:31.510 --> 00:53:32.570
Old man babies.

1404
00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:33.110
Yeah.

1405
00:53:33.650 --> 00:53:34.530
And baby babies.

1406
00:53:35.550 --> 00:53:37.250
These are the three categories of babies.

1407
00:53:37.530 --> 00:53:39.950
You get like a baby that just looks like a baby.

1408
00:53:40.170 --> 00:53:40.950
Just a baby.

1409
00:53:41.190 --> 00:53:41.330
Yeah.

1410
00:53:41.510 --> 00:53:42.370
Just baby baby.

1411
00:53:42.790 --> 00:53:44.770
And then there's like old man baby.

1412
00:53:44.950 --> 00:53:45.210
Yeah.

1413
00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:48.690
Where it's a baby that looks like an old man shrunken down.

1414
00:53:48.870 --> 00:53:49.050
Right.

1415
00:53:49.150 --> 00:53:49.670
So cute.

1416
00:53:49.850 --> 00:53:50.870
And then there's chubber baby.

1417
00:53:51.190 --> 00:53:55.190
Where it's just like rolls and rolls of chub on the baby.

1418
00:53:55.850 --> 00:53:57.650
I can't get enough of all these babies.

1419
00:53:57.810 --> 00:53:58.470
I love all the babies.

1420
00:53:58.510 --> 00:53:58.830
I know.

1421
00:53:58.910 --> 00:53:59.650
They're so cute.

1422
00:54:00.130 --> 00:54:02.830
Well, it's going to be time to breastfeed the baby, which is,

1423
00:54:02.830 --> 00:54:04.610
you know, the senator's cue to leave.

1424
00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:06.050
He's like, ugh, I'm out.

1425
00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:08.510
Dwight wraps the curtain around them.

1426
00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:13.870
And I have to tell you, this scene that I got to do with Rain was one of my most fun

1427
00:54:13.870 --> 00:54:19.070
scenes from being on the show because we knew our characters so well.

1428
00:54:19.210 --> 00:54:21.790
And they had scripted the top half of the scene.

1429
00:54:21.990 --> 00:54:26.970
And then our director, Eric, just let us go and interrupt each other and play around

1430
00:54:26.970 --> 00:54:31.430
until it just felt like a true argument where you're kind of stepping on each other.

1431
00:54:31.430 --> 00:54:34.570
And it didn't feel scripted at all to me.

1432
00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:39.390
Even though I knew the first part of it was, because they let us play around so much with

1433
00:54:39.390 --> 00:54:42.970
it and kept some of our improv in, I just thought the scene turned out so great.

1434
00:54:43.190 --> 00:54:46.570
It is a great example of your chemistry together.

1435
00:54:46.870 --> 00:54:48.590
Like, it just sings.

1436
00:54:49.050 --> 00:54:51.990
Sometimes a scene just sings, and this one did.

1437
00:54:52.190 --> 00:54:53.790
It was so good.

1438
00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:55.070
Thanks, lady.

1439
00:54:55.370 --> 00:54:56.750
I loved doing it.

1440
00:54:56.990 --> 00:55:01.350
You want to guess where the scripted part stopped and we started improvising?

1441
00:55:01.750 --> 00:55:04.150
Did you improvise the line, your face looks like a baby?

1442
00:55:04.410 --> 00:55:06.610
No, that was written.

1443
00:55:06.610 --> 00:55:08.130
And it was such a great line.

1444
00:55:08.930 --> 00:55:13.930
So after Dwight says, and then I kissed you with the force of a thousand waterfalls.

1445
00:55:14.150 --> 00:55:16.590
And Angela says, that didn't happen.

1446
00:55:17.330 --> 00:55:19.470
Everything after that was improvised.

1447
00:55:19.610 --> 00:55:20.410
Oh, amazing.

1448
00:55:20.410 --> 00:55:24.490
When Dwight said, and then I inserted my penis, and I say, no, stop it.

1449
00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:28.290
Like, I had no idea that that was going to be said.

1450
00:55:28.610 --> 00:55:32.330
And then Dwight continues into your, and I'm like, Dwight?

1451
00:55:33.490 --> 00:55:34.590
He goes, vagina, and?

1452
00:55:34.670 --> 00:55:36.790
And I'm like, even if it did, it's a coincidence.

1453
00:55:37.610 --> 00:55:38.810
That was such a great line.

1454
00:55:38.950 --> 00:55:40.090
He's like, there is a chance.

1455
00:55:40.150 --> 00:55:41.230
I was like, he's like, admit it.

1456
00:55:41.290 --> 00:55:42.290
I'm like, I will not.

1457
00:55:42.390 --> 00:55:44.210
All of that was us playing around.

1458
00:55:44.410 --> 00:55:45.710
Oh, it was so good.

1459
00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:48.970
We basically got to play until they sent the senator back in.

1460
00:55:48.970 --> 00:55:53.690
But, you know, we could do that because these characters were so well-written.

1461
00:55:53.850 --> 00:55:55.710
And we knew who they were.

1462
00:55:55.910 --> 00:55:58.290
We knew what they would say to each other, no matter what.

1463
00:55:58.930 --> 00:55:59.130
Yeah.

1464
00:55:59.450 --> 00:56:01.350
That was the magic of the show, right?

1465
00:56:01.570 --> 00:56:01.890
Mm-hmm.

1466
00:56:02.110 --> 00:56:04.990
They could write us so well, and we knew ourselves so well.

1467
00:56:05.450 --> 00:56:08.510
Well, Dwight is going to just burst out of the doors.

1468
00:56:08.750 --> 00:56:11.410
He tells a nurse, cancel the circumcision.

1469
00:56:11.750 --> 00:56:12.090
Yeah.

1470
00:56:12.310 --> 00:56:14.690
I just might be that baby's dad.

1471
00:56:14.990 --> 00:56:17.230
The nurse is like, I don't know what that means.

1472
00:56:17.230 --> 00:56:20.130
By the way, the nurse was played by Carrie Clifford.

1473
00:56:20.230 --> 00:56:23.350
She is a very funny actor, as well as a producer and writer.

1474
00:56:23.550 --> 00:56:26.030
She was absolutely brilliant in this exchange.

1475
00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:27.170
Carrie's hilarious.

1476
00:56:27.550 --> 00:56:30.870
She's one of those people that is funny just reading the phone book.

1477
00:56:31.010 --> 00:56:31.210
Yeah.

1478
00:56:31.370 --> 00:56:33.510
She's just got a very funny delivery.

1479
00:56:33.810 --> 00:56:35.410
She's so very naturally funny.

1480
00:56:35.410 --> 00:56:35.890
I know.

1481
00:56:36.150 --> 00:56:36.510
I know.

1482
00:56:36.730 --> 00:56:36.930
Yeah.

1483
00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:40.510
Should we go back to the office and check in on what's happening there?

1484
00:56:41.310 --> 00:56:41.830
Yes.

1485
00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:45.970
Oh, please, because I have something really fun to share.

1486
00:56:46.430 --> 00:56:51.670
So there is a deleted scene that happens back at the office because Andy is determined

1487
00:56:51.670 --> 00:56:52.970
to keep his lie going.

1488
00:56:53.270 --> 00:56:53.670
Okay.

1489
00:56:53.770 --> 00:56:56.790
Like, he is not going to admit that he lied to help Jim.

1490
00:56:57.170 --> 00:56:58.890
He goes to such great depths.

1491
00:56:59.030 --> 00:56:59.810
Are you ready for this?

1492
00:56:59.910 --> 00:57:00.230
Yes.

1493
00:57:00.230 --> 00:57:03.350
A police officer walks into the office.

1494
00:57:03.870 --> 00:57:07.370
Andy introduces him as Officer Foley from the Scranton PD.

1495
00:57:07.850 --> 00:57:12.210
He said Officer Foley works down at the courthouse, and he has something he'd like to say.

1496
00:57:12.490 --> 00:57:13.830
What is going on?

1497
00:57:14.050 --> 00:57:18.770
Officer Foley says, I understand that some of you don't believe that Mr. Bernard drove

1498
00:57:18.770 --> 00:57:21.390
his friend into the courthouse for jury duty last week.

1499
00:57:21.610 --> 00:57:24.650
Daryl says, correction, we don't care.

1500
00:57:26.270 --> 00:57:28.950
Aaron says, wait, aren't you an actor?

1501
00:57:29.510 --> 00:57:30.330
Andy's like, no.

1502
00:57:30.710 --> 00:57:34.870
Aaron goes, yeah, weren't you in Sweeney Todd last year with Andy?

1503
00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.110
And Officer Foley's like, uh, no.

1504
00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:42.010
And Daryl goes, you hired your actor friend to come here as a cop?

1505
00:57:42.310 --> 00:57:46.390
And Andy says, that is so offensive and ridiculous.

1506
00:57:46.850 --> 00:57:51.990
Daryl says, so if I started singing down by the old mill stream, you couldn't join me

1507
00:57:51.990 --> 00:57:53.110
in a three-part harmony?

1508
00:57:53.110 --> 00:57:55.470
And Andy and Officer Foley are like, no.

1509
00:57:55.750 --> 00:58:01.670
And then Daryl starts singing, and immediately, Andy, Daryl, and Officer Foley have the most

1510
00:58:01.670 --> 00:58:06.230
beautiful harmony, and they sing sort of like a barbershop quartet.

1511
00:58:06.390 --> 00:58:08.950
And at the end, Andy goes, ah, shoot.

1512
00:58:09.310 --> 00:58:13.250
Well, I really think we need to hear Officer Foley, Andy, and Daryl sing.

1513
00:58:13.510 --> 00:58:18.490
And when I traded emails with Aaron Schur about this episode, this was a scene we spoke about.

1514
00:58:18.490 --> 00:58:29.670
Down by the, down by the, down by the old mill stream.

1515
00:58:41.510 --> 00:58:54.630
Gosh, I love that.

1516
00:58:54.790 --> 00:58:55.470
Isn't it great?

1517
00:58:55.650 --> 00:58:58.810
And Andy has such this pure joy reaction.

1518
00:58:58.950 --> 00:58:59.510
He's like, yeah!

1519
00:58:59.790 --> 00:59:01.010
And then he goes, ah, shoot.

1520
00:59:01.590 --> 00:59:02.130
Busted.

1521
00:59:02.910 --> 00:59:05.230
I know who played Officer Foley.

1522
00:59:05.410 --> 00:59:06.410
So do I.

1523
00:59:06.690 --> 00:59:08.990
It was our script supervisor's husband.

1524
00:59:09.250 --> 00:59:12.210
Yes, it was Veda's husband, Michael Tuba Hethernton.

1525
00:59:12.410 --> 00:59:14.550
I mean, Tuba was one of our recurring characters.

1526
00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:16.770
Yes, they sounded so good together.

1527
00:59:16.790 --> 00:59:17.390
I know.

1528
00:59:17.710 --> 00:59:19.130
You know they had fun doing that.

1529
00:59:19.330 --> 00:59:19.890
Oh, my gosh.

1530
00:59:19.990 --> 00:59:22.630
I bet they rehearsed and just were having the best time.

1531
00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:28.230
Well, down in the parking lot, Pam has arrived with the two children.

1532
00:59:28.450 --> 00:59:28.690
Yeah.

1533
00:59:28.990 --> 00:59:31.630
And Jim is helping her unload the kids from the car.

1534
00:59:31.870 --> 00:59:36.450
But then Jim's going to go back up into the office and pretend like he has no idea that

1535
00:59:36.450 --> 00:59:38.850
Pam is there, as if she's surprised.

1536
00:59:39.050 --> 00:59:40.190
Surprise, everybody!

1537
00:59:41.010 --> 00:59:47.030
So I remember something about this moment, Angela, because Pam walks in pushing a stroller

1538
00:59:47.030 --> 00:59:48.210
with baby Phillip.

1539
00:59:48.690 --> 00:59:55.650
And at the table read for this, it was scripted that she was carrying him in his car seat.

1540
00:59:56.250 --> 00:59:58.670
I was on speakerphone for this.

1541
00:59:58.890 --> 01:00:04.330
And I guess afterwards, Angela, you went up to Phil Shea and you said,

1542
01:00:05.090 --> 01:00:06.370
Jedda can't carry car seat.

1543
01:00:06.650 --> 01:00:07.070
No.

1544
01:00:07.310 --> 01:00:08.090
She's not allowed.

1545
01:00:08.410 --> 01:00:09.510
She can't carry that.

1546
01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:10.290
That's too heavy.

1547
01:00:10.390 --> 01:00:11.310
You were like on it.

1548
01:00:11.650 --> 01:00:15.150
You talk about like how the women cared for me in this episode.

1549
01:00:15.150 --> 01:00:15.910
You were like, no, no, no.

1550
01:00:15.910 --> 01:00:17.370
You told everybody she can't do that.

1551
01:00:17.510 --> 01:00:19.050
She has to have a stroller.

1552
01:00:19.290 --> 01:00:19.490
Yes.

1553
01:00:19.630 --> 01:00:24.630
Because Phil Shea called me and he said, Jenna, is it true that you can't carry a car seat?

1554
01:00:24.730 --> 01:00:25.510
I said, that's right.

1555
01:00:25.750 --> 01:00:27.570
I'm still in that phase of postpartum.

1556
01:00:28.250 --> 01:00:28.510
You had stitches.

1557
01:00:28.990 --> 01:00:30.110
I had stitches still.

1558
01:00:30.230 --> 01:00:31.090
They hadn't been removed.

1559
01:00:31.350 --> 01:00:33.990
And you can't carry more than like a bag of flour, right?

1560
01:00:34.110 --> 01:00:34.770
That's what they say.

1561
01:00:34.830 --> 01:00:36.470
You can carry your baby and that's about it.

1562
01:00:36.670 --> 01:00:36.770
Yeah.

1563
01:00:37.010 --> 01:00:39.090
So he said, can you push a stroller?

1564
01:00:39.510 --> 01:00:41.010
I said, I can push a stroller.

1565
01:00:41.210 --> 01:00:45.910
But I'll tell you what, I'm pushing that stroller with one arm because I was holding

1566
01:00:45.910 --> 01:00:47.510
on to the hand of little Cece.

1567
01:00:47.790 --> 01:00:51.590
And that was not easy, actually, at that point.

1568
01:00:52.050 --> 01:00:52.410
Yeah.

1569
01:00:52.470 --> 01:00:53.230
Because you're getting cold.

1570
01:00:53.230 --> 01:00:55.890
The one-handed push and then the, it was, yeah.

1571
01:00:56.230 --> 01:00:56.490
Yeah.

1572
01:00:56.590 --> 01:01:02.050
I went up to Phil Shea and was like, and I didn't feel like I needed to announce it to the room

1573
01:01:02.050 --> 01:01:03.530
because I knew Phil would just make it happen.

1574
01:01:03.610 --> 01:01:04.470
He would just take care of me.

1575
01:01:04.550 --> 01:01:04.650
Yeah.

1576
01:01:04.730 --> 01:01:05.090
He did.

1577
01:01:05.090 --> 01:01:06.590
Well, guess what?

1578
01:01:07.150 --> 01:01:09.110
Cece made everyone a picture.

1579
01:01:09.430 --> 01:01:10.730
This made me laugh so hard.

1580
01:01:10.910 --> 01:01:16.390
I cracked up when Ryan is like, why am I smaller than the desk?

1581
01:01:16.570 --> 01:01:18.510
And then the look between Jim and Pam.

1582
01:01:18.630 --> 01:01:18.990
Uh-huh.

1583
01:01:19.250 --> 01:01:20.090
I know.

1584
01:01:20.270 --> 01:01:23.030
Because clearly, Pam has made these drawings.

1585
01:01:23.130 --> 01:01:23.570
Yes.

1586
01:01:23.910 --> 01:01:24.430
Yeah.

1587
01:01:24.870 --> 01:01:26.410
And they're handing them out.

1588
01:01:26.410 --> 01:01:31.170
And I love, I love when Kelly is like, did you make this drawing?

1589
01:01:31.350 --> 01:01:32.810
And Cece's like, no.

1590
01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:33.290
Uh-huh.

1591
01:01:33.390 --> 01:01:34.970
And Pam's like, she says no to everything.

1592
01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:35.410
Uh-huh.

1593
01:01:35.410 --> 01:01:36.550
She loves to say no.

1594
01:01:38.650 --> 01:01:42.970
Well, it's very clear that Cece did not make these drawings.

1595
01:01:43.210 --> 01:01:46.090
And then everything sort of erupts into chaos.

1596
01:01:46.490 --> 01:01:46.630
Yeah.

1597
01:01:46.950 --> 01:01:48.390
Cece starts crying.

1598
01:01:48.790 --> 01:01:48.910
Mm-hmm.

1599
01:01:48.910 --> 01:01:50.750
And then the baby starts crying.

1600
01:01:50.750 --> 01:01:56.210
And then I thought Daryl had the best talking head when he was like, look, I'm a dad.

1601
01:01:56.710 --> 01:01:59.250
Those five days at home for Jim, that was no vacation.

1602
01:01:59.830 --> 01:02:00.010
Yeah.

1603
01:02:00.370 --> 01:02:01.570
Everyone just gets it.

1604
01:02:01.910 --> 01:02:03.990
We got a fan question from Jesse W.

1605
01:02:04.090 --> 01:02:07.430
in Alabama and many others who wanted to know how we filmed

1606
01:02:07.430 --> 01:02:10.410
the scene with Cece being upset and crying.

1607
01:02:10.770 --> 01:02:10.970
Yeah.

1608
01:02:10.970 --> 01:02:12.590
How does that work in show business?

1609
01:02:12.870 --> 01:02:12.990
Yeah.

1610
01:02:13.230 --> 01:02:17.830
Well, basically what happened was her mom had been on set with us while we were filming,

1611
01:02:17.830 --> 01:02:22.150
and her mom stepped away for a second, and she started to cry, and we captured it.

1612
01:02:22.710 --> 01:02:25.030
And then her mom came on very quickly.

1613
01:02:25.230 --> 01:02:26.790
There's a lot of movie magic involved.

1614
01:02:27.010 --> 01:02:30.730
Like, you might notice, like, when she's running around in the conference room, you

1615
01:02:30.730 --> 01:02:31.990
only see the back of her head.

1616
01:02:32.110 --> 01:02:32.310
Yeah.

1617
01:02:32.350 --> 01:02:33.650
She was not upset.

1618
01:02:34.150 --> 01:02:34.250
Right.

1619
01:02:34.330 --> 01:02:38.430
She was actually having fun, and we added the crying into the background.

1620
01:02:38.670 --> 01:02:38.830
Yeah.

1621
01:02:38.990 --> 01:02:39.870
Same with Philip.

1622
01:02:40.090 --> 01:02:43.670
Like, Philip's pacifier fell out of his mouth for a second, and he started fussing,

1623
01:02:43.690 --> 01:02:47.550
and I basically put it right back in, and then they just put crying noises on it.

1624
01:02:47.550 --> 01:02:47.710
Yeah.

1625
01:02:48.010 --> 01:02:50.510
But Cece was upset for a second.

1626
01:02:50.810 --> 01:02:50.950
Yeah.

1627
01:02:51.070 --> 01:02:55.370
And you can see me in the scene reach down because I'm her mom.

1628
01:02:55.710 --> 01:02:55.990
Yeah.

1629
01:02:56.070 --> 01:02:57.070
You know, fictionally.

1630
01:02:57.270 --> 01:02:59.330
And I normally would have picked her up.

1631
01:02:59.550 --> 01:02:59.850
Yeah.

1632
01:02:59.970 --> 01:03:00.590
But you couldn't.

1633
01:03:00.630 --> 01:03:01.650
I couldn't pick her up.

1634
01:03:01.850 --> 01:03:01.990
Yeah.

1635
01:03:02.030 --> 01:03:02.230
Yeah.

1636
01:03:02.370 --> 01:03:06.930
Because those little girls, I've said before, they were played by Bailey and Sienna Stroll.

1637
01:03:07.370 --> 01:03:09.390
We spent a lot of time with them, John and I.

1638
01:03:09.610 --> 01:03:09.870
Yeah.

1639
01:03:10.030 --> 01:03:12.890
That's what you do when you're working with a baby or a toddler.

1640
01:03:13.090 --> 01:03:13.750
You go early.

1641
01:03:13.890 --> 01:03:14.770
You get to know the parents.

1642
01:03:14.770 --> 01:03:19.090
You spend time with them so that when the cameras are rolling, you've had a little time

1643
01:03:19.090 --> 01:03:20.110
to get to know each other.

1644
01:03:20.270 --> 01:03:21.810
They were very familiar with us.

1645
01:03:21.970 --> 01:03:22.110
Yeah.

1646
01:03:22.250 --> 01:03:24.770
And if I could have, I would have just swooped down and picked her up.

1647
01:03:24.870 --> 01:03:26.130
And they loved us.

1648
01:03:26.210 --> 01:03:28.250
We had a really good relationship with them.

1649
01:03:28.390 --> 01:03:28.970
I know.

1650
01:03:29.150 --> 01:03:30.130
They were so sweet.

1651
01:03:30.550 --> 01:03:33.810
So, Jemma's going to walk Pam and the kids out, and everyone's like, you know what?

1652
01:03:33.890 --> 01:03:34.890
Just go home, buddy.

1653
01:03:35.450 --> 01:03:35.590
Yeah.

1654
01:03:35.590 --> 01:03:36.550
Just go home.

1655
01:03:36.950 --> 01:03:39.650
Everyone kind of forgives him in this moment.

1656
01:03:39.650 --> 01:03:41.810
When they see what he was really home with all week.

1657
01:03:42.070 --> 01:03:42.330
Yeah.

1658
01:03:42.730 --> 01:03:45.050
I have something kind of juicy to share.

1659
01:03:45.270 --> 01:03:45.730
What?

1660
01:03:46.570 --> 01:03:54.810
Well, in the shooting draft, there was a must-shoot talking head with Kathy and a must-shoot alt.

1661
01:03:55.670 --> 01:03:57.230
I'm going to read them both to you.

1662
01:03:57.350 --> 01:03:58.450
That would have gone right here?

1663
01:03:58.590 --> 01:03:58.990
Yeah.

1664
01:03:59.310 --> 01:03:59.630
Okay.

1665
01:03:59.990 --> 01:04:00.430
Ready?

1666
01:04:00.430 --> 01:04:01.030
Yes.

1667
01:04:01.650 --> 01:04:04.610
Oh, the message boards would have gone crazy.

1668
01:04:05.410 --> 01:04:10.890
So, there would have been a little spy shot here of Kathy talking on her cell phone to

1669
01:04:10.890 --> 01:04:11.650
one of her friends.

1670
01:04:12.550 --> 01:04:13.070
Really?

1671
01:04:13.350 --> 01:04:13.790
Uh-huh.

1672
01:04:14.090 --> 01:04:15.230
Guess what she says.

1673
01:04:15.630 --> 01:04:16.150
What?

1674
01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:20.030
I'm out of a job as soon as Pam gets tired of those brats.

1675
01:04:20.330 --> 01:04:22.430
Hey, maybe I should be her nanny.

1676
01:04:22.690 --> 01:04:23.930
My friend's a nanny.

1677
01:04:24.150 --> 01:04:25.470
We could hang out at the park together.

1678
01:04:25.730 --> 01:04:26.650
Oh, my God.

1679
01:04:26.650 --> 01:04:27.730
I went to college.

1680
01:04:28.710 --> 01:04:29.030
Hmm.

1681
01:04:29.030 --> 01:04:31.470
And are you ready for the alt?

1682
01:04:31.750 --> 01:04:32.630
What's the alt?

1683
01:04:32.930 --> 01:04:34.690
Must-shoot alt.

1684
01:04:35.130 --> 01:04:36.790
There's footage of this somewhere.

1685
01:04:37.650 --> 01:04:43.210
Kathy would have said on the phone, I hadn't seen it before, but Jim kind of has that sexy

1686
01:04:43.210 --> 01:04:47.450
dad thing going on if, like, you're into sexy dads.

1687
01:04:49.410 --> 01:04:50.850
I have to say something.

1688
01:04:51.070 --> 01:04:51.550
What?

1689
01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:56.150
I feel like they did not know what to do with this character of Kathy.

1690
01:04:56.150 --> 01:04:57.170
No, they didn't.

1691
01:04:57.170 --> 01:05:05.030
Because for the trivia episode, they had written a whole thing where Pam invited Kathy over

1692
01:05:05.030 --> 01:05:06.150
to meet the new baby.

1693
01:05:06.370 --> 01:05:07.390
To her house.

1694
01:05:07.590 --> 01:05:07.850
Yes.

1695
01:05:07.990 --> 01:05:10.230
And now you have this thing going on.

1696
01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:10.690
Mm-hmm.

1697
01:05:10.830 --> 01:05:16.890
I feel like there's this really big gap in the story of Kathy, this character.

1698
01:05:17.190 --> 01:05:17.310
Yeah.

1699
01:05:17.310 --> 01:05:22.010
And I'm really interested to get into it with Lindsay because that episode of Pam's replacement

1700
01:05:22.010 --> 01:05:23.110
is great.

1701
01:05:23.110 --> 01:05:24.410
It's a great idea.

1702
01:05:24.650 --> 01:05:25.770
She kills it.

1703
01:05:25.870 --> 01:05:26.590
It's so fun.

1704
01:05:26.770 --> 01:05:29.130
And then basically, she's just in the background.

1705
01:05:29.390 --> 01:05:29.870
Yeah.

1706
01:05:30.130 --> 01:05:32.910
Until Tallahassee when she throws herself at Jim.

1707
01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:36.590
It's very—it makes her seem cray-cray.

1708
01:05:37.090 --> 01:05:41.550
But the truth is, is that there were all these little things, like, what is she?

1709
01:05:41.750 --> 01:05:43.290
Is she friends with Jim and Pam?

1710
01:05:43.550 --> 01:05:44.710
Is she hot for Jim?

1711
01:05:45.210 --> 01:05:51.930
Like, they were throwing a lot at this character, and I feel like never really found her purpose.

1712
01:05:52.350 --> 01:05:52.510
Right.

1713
01:05:52.770 --> 01:05:53.230
Yeah.

1714
01:05:53.230 --> 01:05:58.110
I'm excited to talk to Lindsay about it, too, because her being a foil to Pam, I get.

1715
01:05:58.390 --> 01:05:58.730
Mm-hmm.

1716
01:05:58.730 --> 01:06:00.210
You know, I get that idea.

1717
01:06:00.750 --> 01:06:05.350
But then they never set her up to do that correctly as a character.

1718
01:06:05.710 --> 01:06:08.570
Yeah, like, they wrote things for her, but they were never aired.

1719
01:06:08.850 --> 01:06:11.630
So the Tallahassee thing really comes out of the blue.

1720
01:06:11.810 --> 01:06:11.990
Yeah.

1721
01:06:12.170 --> 01:06:16.070
But what people should know is that it was there, it was being written, but it wasn't making

1722
01:06:16.070 --> 01:06:17.090
it into the episodes.

1723
01:06:17.290 --> 01:06:17.490
Yeah.

1724
01:06:18.170 --> 01:06:24.890
Well, this episode ends with Dwight bursting into the office, smoking a cigar, and he has

1725
01:06:24.890 --> 01:06:25.730
a talking head.

1726
01:06:26.210 --> 01:06:26.650
Yeah.

1727
01:06:26.650 --> 01:06:27.570
He forgives Jim.

1728
01:06:27.570 --> 01:06:28.750
Um, he does.

1729
01:06:29.030 --> 01:06:29.750
You know why?

1730
01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:31.610
He was doing it for his kids.

1731
01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:32.030
Mm-hmm.

1732
01:06:32.250 --> 01:06:34.750
And us dads, you know?

1733
01:06:34.950 --> 01:06:36.570
He says, us dads.

1734
01:06:37.550 --> 01:06:38.070
Yeah.

1735
01:06:39.070 --> 01:06:40.010
He's a dad.

1736
01:06:40.190 --> 01:06:41.130
He's a dad.

1737
01:06:41.550 --> 01:06:42.890
Or so he thinks.

1738
01:06:43.650 --> 01:06:44.630
Or is he?

1739
01:06:45.070 --> 01:06:46.090
No spoilers!

1740
01:06:46.230 --> 01:06:49.010
What if this is someone's first time watching the show?

1741
01:06:49.010 --> 01:06:49.830
No spoilers.

1742
01:06:50.250 --> 01:06:53.350
All right, I won't say anything, but we'll get there.

1743
01:06:53.590 --> 01:06:54.390
Yeah, we will.

1744
01:06:54.630 --> 01:06:55.950
Well, that was Jury Duty.

1745
01:06:55.950 --> 01:06:58.570
I loved watching this episode.

1746
01:06:58.850 --> 01:07:00.590
I'm so excited Pam's back.

1747
01:07:00.830 --> 01:07:04.350
I want to give a big thank you to Steve Burgess and Aaron Schur.

1748
01:07:04.530 --> 01:07:05.550
We both reached out.

1749
01:07:05.730 --> 01:07:08.910
Yes, and Halstead for answering my question about his name.

1750
01:07:08.910 --> 01:07:12.210
And to all of you for writing in with your great catches and questions.

1751
01:07:12.570 --> 01:07:13.750
You guys have a great week.

1752
01:07:13.910 --> 01:07:14.870
We'll see you next week.

1753
01:07:14.970 --> 01:07:15.350
See you then.

1754
01:07:19.880 --> 01:07:21.700
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

